From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 14:29:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] AP MLD: Run EAP of a non-MLO station on its own link

Since commit a213fee11da3 ("AP: MLO: Make IEEE 802.1X SM, authserv, and
RADIUS client singletons"), the links of an AP MLD share the IEEE 802.1X
authenticator, the EAP server configuration and the RADIUS client of the
first BSS, and these call back with the first BSS. A non-MLO station has
an entry only on the link it associated to. For such a station on
another link:
- ieee802_1x_sta_entry_alive() does not find the station, so its state
  machine never starts EAP,
- EAPOL frames would leave on the link of the first BSS,
- the EAP user lookup uses the WPS state and AP PIN of the first BSS,
  EAP-WSC uses its registrar, and the EAP events go to its control
  interface,
- RADIUS requests carry the attributes of the first BSS, and
  ieee802_1x_receive_auth() drops each reply, since it searches only
  the stations of the first BSS,
- an EAP-SIM database reply and RADIUS DAS Disconnect and CoA requests
  search only the same stations and PMKSA cache.
A WPS Enrollee or an IEEE 802.1X station on a link other than the first
never completes EAP.

Let the authenticator callbacks act on the link whose entry is the
station of the state machine, and accept a state machine of any link as
alive. Callbacks for an MLO station and for a BSS outside an AP MLD keep
the BSS of the callback.

Search the stations of all links for the request of a RADIUS reply, for
an EAP-SIM database session and for a RADIUS DAS request, and the PMKSA
caches of all links for a DAS Disconnect. For DAS, skip the entries of
an MLO station on links other than its association link, so that one
MLO station counts as one session. Give each EAP session the WPS
context and the control interface of the link of the station. The
RADIUS server gives its sessions those of its EAP server configuration,
as before.

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
---

--- a/src/ap/authsrv.c
+++ b/src/ap/authsrv.c
